\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Path pairings and free orbit coordinates}
\date{2026-11-19}

\p{For any \vocabk{orbit section}{fdip-010X}, the
\vocabk{framed path transport}{fdip-011B} is the ordered product
#{\check U_\gamma} of the reduced links on the path. Given a #{G}-invariant #{k}-bilinear
pairing, the \vocabk{scalar path pairing}{fdip-011B} is gauge invariant, with
##{\langle\phi_x,U_\gamma\phi_y\rangle
   =\langle\phi^\circ,\check U_\gamma\phi^\circ\rangle.}
For a free scalar orbit the group-valued framed transports are gauge
invariant. Their values on the chosen oriented edges identify the set of
joint gauge orbits bijectively with #{G^E}.}

\proof{
\p{On a positive traversal insert the head frame inverse on the
left and the source frame on the right. On a backward traversal
invert that entire expression. Consecutive frames cancel, leaving
#{g_x^{-1}U_\gamma g_y}, including the identity for the empty path.
Use #{\phi_x=g_x\phi^\circ} and invariance of the pairing to move
#{g_x} across as its inverse. Under a vertex gauge #{a} the path
transport itself becomes #{a_xU_\gamma a_y^{-1}}, by the same
cancellation. Both arguments of the original pairing therefore
transform by #{a_x}, preserving the scalar.}

\p{For a free orbit the frame of #{a_x\phi_x} is #{a_xg_x} by
uniqueness. The endpoint gauges now cancel from the framed group
element. Each path consisting of one chosen oriented edge gives its reduced link;
the residual vertex group in \ref{fdip-0112} is trivial. Every
link tuple occurs with the constant reference scalar, proving the
claimed bijection.}
}

\p{A bare plaquette holonomy based at #{x} changes to
#{a_x U_f a_x^{-1}} by \ref{fdip-010C}. Taking a class function
gives a scalar observable. The group element itself can change
when the holonomy is noncentral.}
